  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 AM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nThe story today (and for the next few days, as it has been for \nthe past two and a half months) is monsoonal moisture. Satellite\nimagery shows an area of moisture advancing northward with \nshowers and embedded thunderstorms moving toward Sweetwater \nCounty as I write this around 1 am. This area of showers will \nspread northward tonight. Meanwhile, most of northern Wyoming \nshould remain dry tonight.\n\nThis deeper moisture will overspread the entire area today, with \nprecipitable water levels rising to 150 to 175 percent of normal. \nJust about all areas will have at least a 1 in 6 chance of a shower \nor thunderstorm today. The best chances will be across western \nWyoming where there will be more forcing, and also across areas \neast of the Bighorns, with the least chance in the center, \nespecially the central basins. There continues to be one factor \nthat makes me think coverage of thunderstorms may be less \nthough. There will be a decent amount of cloud cover around the \narea, and this could limit surface instability somewhat. And as\nit is we are now almost to the autumnal equinox, not the middle\nof summer. So, the sun is weaker and daytime heating will be \nless. This may also limit the chance of heavy rain and any \nflooding potential. Steering flow is less than yesterday, but we\nstill have 10 to 20 knots and this should be enough to keep \nstorms moving somewhat. However, with the high precipitable \nwaters there could still be isolated minor flooding, especially \nwest of the Continental Divide. Most showers should end after \nsunset, but a few showers will likely linger through the night. \nAnd it will probably be more than expected, the models have been\nhorrible this season with nocturnal monsoonal convection.\n\nAn upper level low that has been helping to funnel the moisture into \nthe area will finally begin to move eastward today, drifting \ntoward Idaho by tomorrow. This may be the most active day for \nconvection of the period, especially thunderstorms. Most \nguidance is showing a clearer sky in the morning, allowing for \nmore daytime heating and surface instability. Instability \nparameters are decent for this time of year, with CAPE \napproaching 700 J/kg and lifted indices as low as minus 4. With\nthe approaching upper level flow, there will also be some \ndecent directional shear, especially in western Wyoming. The \nresult will be a chance for some stronger thunderstorms, again \nespecially in western Wyoming where there is a Marginal risk of\nsevere weather. The main threat would be hail given the low wet\nbulb zero levels. With more sun and southwest flow continuing, \nthis will also be a warmer day, especially east of the Divide \nwhere warmer spots may get close to 80 for a high. Showers will \ncontinue into the night as well. With the added instability and \na shortwave rotating around the low, if there are any flooding \nproblems, this would be the time for it.\n\nThe latest guidance continues to slow the progression of the low, \nonly slowly moving it over Wyoming by Saturday. On this day, \nthe best chance of showers and storms would be across northern \nWyoming. Most guidance is in decent agreement on this. The \ndeepest moisture would be over Montana, but most areas will \nhave a chance. There may also be a dry slot that moves in \nportions of the area, but exactly where is still in question. \nThe low will slowly cross the state on Sunday, but will still \nbe close enough for some wrap around moisture to bring more \nshowers, mainly to northern and eastern portions of the area. \nThis also looks like a cooler day as flow turns to the \nnorthwest, with temperatures anywhere from 5 to 10 degrees \ncooler than on Saturday. Following this, there is more \ndivergence in the models with some bringing another system \nacross the area early next week with another chance of showers \nand others suppressing it to the south, keeping the area largely\nrain free. All guidance shows south to southwesterly flow \nreturning though, so temperatures look to be back above normal \nas we head into the start of autumn, which arrives on Tuesday, \nSeptember 22nd at 6:04 pm.",
